    "A": "Develop the strongest low-overhead serial stencil. Replace separate row allocations with contiguous double buffers, hoist row addresses, and eliminate the interior copy by swapping buffers. Initialize both buffers' boundaries to the original boundary values; blindly retaining new_grid's zero boundaries is incorrect for tiny grids. Ensure output uses the latest committed buffer. Pursue SIMD-friendly loops with valid aliasing assumptions and scalar tails, preserving the original addition order. Compare a vectorizable Boolean-OR convergence reduction with a version that stops checking differences once an excessive difference is found, while still computing every remaining update. Measure storage/swapping and convergence/SIMD changes separately under the unchanged harness. Preserve empty-interior and zero-iteration behavior.",
    "B": "Investigate CPU parallelism using only threading facilities supported by the unchanged build. Compare one, two and four total computing threads, never exceeding the four-core allocation. Use a persistent team over contiguous row bands rather than creating workers every iteration. Each iteration must finish all stencil updates, combine private convergence flags with OR, and synchronize before consuming the new buffer or stopping. Avoid false sharing in worker flags. Establish a generic serial fallback for workloads too small to amortize synchronization and startup, based on runtime work rather than test identities. Measure against a comparable serial kernel; neither four-core scaling nor a gain over A is guaranteed. Do not change compiler or linker settings to enable an unavailable runtime.",
    "C": "Research complementary work avoidance and output costs, with separate measurements. First, exploit exact finite propagation from the initialized hot rectangle: after t updates, nonzero interior values can only occur within its t-cell dependency expansion. A clipped rectangular superset is sufficient. Expand the computed region every iteration, include its new frontier in convergence checking, keep inactive buffer entries zero, and preserve reference boundary values. Use a safe full-domain path for degenerate grids and once the active region fills the interior. Never discard small nonzero values or skip convergence decisions. Second, investigate larger stdout buffering and row/chunk aggregation using the same C floating-point conversion. Preserve six decimal places, the space after every value and the newline after every row; do not substitute approximate formatting or omit output. Evaluate initialization and formatting costs as part of total runtime.",
    "G": "Integrate only measured, correct programs produced by the research trees. Start with the fastest validated standalone candidate, retaining the baseline as a fallback. Add complementary changes incrementally rather than merging everything at once. Reconcile buffer ownership, stride, initialized boundaries, active-region bounds, SIMD tails, convergence reductions, synchronization and final-buffer selection. In particular, active-region restriction may change the threading break-even point. Evaluate each integrated artifact through the unchanged automatic harness within existing budgets, recording its source identity, correctness and end-to-end timing. Compare combinations against their strongest individual ingredient, not against an assumed sum of speedups. Reject numerical/output mismatches and measured regressions; the final selection may legitimately be a single-tree program.",
    "analysis": "The relevant task is Problem E in src/laplace_seq.c. The baseline passed four correctness cases and averaged 0.068681987 seconds, with samples spanning approximately 0.068299–0.069390 seconds. No workload dimensions or phase timings are supplied, so memory traffic, convergence bookkeeping, threading overhead and formatting are hypotheses to measure, not established bottlenecks. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. All trees must preserve initialization, fixed boundaries, double-precision stencil evaluation in the original east-plus-west-plus-north-plus-south order, and the strict comparison against the original 1.0e-5f constant promoted to double. Preserve the iteration cap and commit the update that first satisfies convergence. Four passing cases do not establish correctness for every valid input. Keep tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target and permissions unchanged; use no expected-answer substitution or test-specific specialization."
